---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="waf-sensitive-data — rhocfsandbox" active="demos">
  <h1><span class="prompt">demo: sensitive data detection (sdd)</span></h1>
  <p class="dim">
    two path prefixes, same synthetic payloads, different waf actions. cloudflare
    inspects response bodies at egress against the sensitive data managed ruleset
    (ssn, credit card, api key, email, phone), then either logs a finding or
    blocks the response. all values below are synthetic — reserved-invalid or
    industry test values.
  </p>

  <h2># log endpoints</h2>
  <p class="dim">
    return the payload, sdd matches, finding shows up in security events. no
    visible impact to the client.
  </p>
  <div class="grid">
    <a href="/api/sensitive-log/ssn" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-log/ssn</h3><p class="dim">synthetic ssn (reserved-invalid <code>000-xx</code> range)</p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-log/cc" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-log/cc</h3><p class="dim">industry test visa <code>4111 1111 1111 1111</code></p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-log/email" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-log/email</h3><p class="dim">example.com address</p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-log/phone" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-log/phone</h3><p class="dim">reserved <code>555-01xx</code> range</p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-log/apikey" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-log/apikey</h3><p class="dim">plausibly-shaped fake token</p></a>
  </div>

  <h2># block endpoints</h2>
  <p class="dim">
    same payloads, but the sdd rule action here is <code>block</code>. the origin
    function still runs, cloudflare inspects the response, matches the detector,
    and replaces the body with a block page before it leaves the edge.
  </p>
  <div class="grid">
    <a href="/api/sensitive-block/ssn" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-block/ssn</h3><p class="dim">expect <code>403</code></p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-block/cc" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-block/cc</h3><p class="dim">expect <code>403</code></p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-block/email" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-block/email</h3><p class="dim">expect <code>403</code></p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-block/phone" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-block/phone</h3><p class="dim">expect <code>403</code></p></a>
    <a href="/api/sensitive-block/apikey" class="card"><h3>GET /api/sensitive-block/apikey</h3><p class="dim">expect <code>403</code></p></a>
  </div>

  <h2># from curl</h2>
  <pre><code># log path — should return 200 with the synthetic payload
curl -i https://rhocfsandbox.com/api/sensitive-log/cc

# block path — should return 403 with cloudflare's block body
curl -i https://rhocfsandbox.com/api/sensitive-block/cc</code></pre>

  <h2># where to look on the cloudflare side</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>security → security events</strong>: filter by ruleset "sensitive data detection"</li>
    <li><strong>security → wAF → managed rulesets</strong>: deployment overrides for log vs block</li>
    <li><strong>analytics → security analytics</strong>: sdd findings show up as a category</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout chill">
    <strong>the talking point:</strong> sdd is body inspection at egress. same
    engine as zero trust dlp, but applied to public web responses instead of
    warp-tunneled traffic. useful when your api accidentally returns pii, or
    when a compromised endpoint starts leaking.
  </div>
</Layout>
